# Secrets — never commit
.env

# The session signing key, minted on first run by auth.resolve_secret_key()
# when neither QMS_SECRET_KEY nor SECRET_KEY is set. Committing it would
# publish the value that signs every session cookie, which is the whole of what
# the old hardcoded demo default got wrong (ABOUT.md §7 gap 8).
secret_key.txt

# Workbooks. The ones under fixtures/ and demo_files/ are SYNTHETIC — every
# name and figure fictional — and are committed; fixtures/build_fixtures.py
# rebuilds them. A real customer's workbook never belongs in this repository:
# keep it outside the checkout (customer_docs/ is ignored for exactly that).
customer_docs/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Database dumps — confidential, and large. The folder is not tracked at all;
# tools/backup_db.py creates it the first time it writes a backup.
backups/

# Attachments — CC-2 B8. Real files: a scanned supplier bill, a bank slip, a
# cheque image. Commercial data belonging to the company, written at runtime by
# attachment.py and served by nothing. Same reasoning as backups/ above, and
# the same convention: a gitignored runtime directory at the repository root.
# ⚠ Overridable with ATTACHMENT_DIR, so a deployment can put it on another
#   volume. The tests point it at a temporary directory and never write here.
attachments/

# Timestamped editor backups — `ABOUT.md.bak.20260809-1305`, `ra.py.bak.…-corr`.
# They were untracked but NOT ignored, so every `git status` listed twenty of
# them and a `git add -A` would have committed the lot. Git history is the
# revert point; these are not.
*.bak
*.bak.*

Quote.html
# A customer's own files, under the old folder name too. Never commit one.
client_docs/

# Dated scratch notes — `31AUG.md`, `2SEP.md`. A day's working notes, written
# to the repository root and named after the date. They are not part of the
# document set: INTRODUCTION.md §4 is the map, every file on it is deliberate,
# and a 36-byte note reading "Updated Measurement Sheet" is a reminder to its
# author rather than a record anyone else can read. Git history is where a
# change is explained; PROGRESS.md is where a build state is recorded.
# ⚠ They were untracked but NOT ignored, so every `git status --porcelain`
#   reported the tree dirty and each pass had to take the same judgement call
#   about a file nobody meant to commit. Same reasoning as `*.bak` above.
[0-9][A-Z][A-Z][A-Z].md
[0-9][0-9][A-Z][A-Z][A-Z].md
